/* data/learn-c/phy-12-07.js - GENERATED by tools/build_learn.py from data/learn/summaries/phy-12-07.json (our own words;
 * the notes of one chapter, loaded when the chapter is opened). Do not edit by hand. */
(window.LEARN_C = window.LEARN_C || {})["phy-12-07"] = {"7.1":{"s":"Ray (geometrical) optics treats light as travelling in straight lines and explains reflection, refraction and images by mirrors and lenses. It works because the wavelength of light is tiny compared with mirrors, lenses and obstacles. Wave optics treats light as a wave and also explains interference, diffraction and polarisation.","f":[{"x":"n_1*sin(i) = n_2*sin(r)","n":"Snell's law; n_1, n_2 = absolute refractive indices of the two media.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 7.1, p.158"}]},"7.2":{"s":"Newton's corpuscular theory said light is a stream of tiny particles. It explained straight-line travel, reflection and refraction, but predicted light is faster in a denser medium, which experiment disproved. Huygens' wave theory explained reflection, refraction, and later interference, diffraction and polarisation. Maxwell showed light is an electromagnetic wave. Today light is known to have a dual nature: wave and particle (photons).","w":["Corpuscular theory: light faster in a denser medium. Wave theory: slower. Experiment agrees with the wave theory."]},"7.3":{"s":"Light is a transverse electromagnetic wave: electric and magnetic fields oscillate at right angles to each other and to the direction of travel. It needs no medium. Its speed in a medium depends on the refractive index. Visible light covers about 400-700 nm, violet shortest and red longest. The refractive index depends on wavelength, which is why white light splits into colours in a prism.","f":[{"x":"n = c/v","n":"Refractive index: speed of light in vacuum over speed in the medium.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 7.3, p.159"}]},"7.4":{"s":"Primary sources make their own light (Sun, flame, bulb); secondary sources only reflect or scatter it (Moon, objects around us). A wavefront is the surface joining points that vibrate in the same phase; rays are perpendicular to it. A point source gives spherical wavefronts, a distant source plane ones. Huygens' principle: every point on a wavefront acts as a source of secondary wavelets, and the forward envelope of these wavelets is the new wavefront.","w":["Backward wavelets are simply ignored in Huygens' theory; the theory is empirical."]},"7.5":{"s":"Using Huygens' wavelets for a plane wavefront hitting a mirror, the two triangles formed are congruent, which proves the laws of reflection: the angle of incidence equals the angle of reflection, and the incident ray, reflected ray and normal lie in one plane. The same construction explains lateral inversion of the image in a plane mirror.","w":["Reflection does not change the speed, wavelength or frequency of the light."]},"7.6":{"s":"With Huygens' wavelets travelling at different speeds in two media, the refracted wavefront gives Snell's law. Light slows down in a denser medium and bends towards the normal. In going from one medium to another the frequency stays the same, while the speed and wavelength both change in the same ratio.","f":[{"x":"sin(i)/sin(r) = v_1/v_2 = n_2/n_1","n":"Snell's law from Huygens' construction.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 7.6, p.163"},{"x":"lambda = lambda_0/n","n":"Wavelength in a medium of refractive index n; lambda_0 = wavelength in vacuum.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 7.6, p.164"}],"w":["Frequency (and so colour) does not change on refraction; wavelength and speed do."]},"7.7":{"s":"In ordinary (unpolarised) light the electric field vibrates in all directions perpendicular to the ray. A polariser such as a polaroid lets through only the part along its axis, giving plane polarised light at half the intensity. A second polariser (analyser) passes a fraction cos²θ of that. Reflected light is completely polarised at Brewster's angle, when reflected and refracted rays are at 90°. Scattered sunlight is also partly polarised. Only transverse waves can be polarised.","f":[{"x":"I = I_1*cos^2(theta)","n":"Malus' law; theta = angle between the axes of the two polarisers, I_1 = intensity after the first.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 7.7, p.166"},{"x":"tan(theta_B) = n_2/n_1","n":"Brewster's law; at this angle theta_B + theta_r = 90 degrees.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 7.7, p.167"}],"w":["Unpolarised light loses half its intensity at the first polariser whatever its orientation.","Sound cannot be polarised: it is a longitudinal wave."]},"7.8":{"s":"When two coherent waves (same frequency, constant phase difference) overlap, bright fringes appear where they meet in phase and dark fringes where they meet in opposite phase. In Young's double slit experiment two slits lit from one source act as coherent sources and give equally spaced bright and dark fringes. Lloyd's mirror and Fresnel's biprism are other ways to get coherent sources. A distance d in a medium of index n counts as an optical path nd.","f":[{"x":"Delta l = y*d/D","n":"Path difference at height y on the screen; d = slit separation, D = slits-to-screen distance.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 7.8, p.170"},{"x":"y_n = n*lambda*D/d","n":"Position of the n-th bright fringe; dark fringes at (n - 1/2) lambda D/d.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 7.8, p.170"},{"x":"W = lambda*D/d","n":"Fringe width: same for bright and dark fringes.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 7.8, p.170"},{"x":"Delta phi = (2*pi/lambda)*Delta l","n":"Phase difference from path difference.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 7.8, p.171"},{"x":"I = 4*I_0*cos^2(phi/2)","n":"Intensity where the phase difference is phi; I_0 = intensity of each wave.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 7.8, p.171"},{"x":"Delta l = (n - 1)*t","n":"Extra path added by a thin plate of thickness t and index n in front of one slit; the pattern shifts.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 7.8, p.175"}],"w":["Two separate bulbs are not coherent: no steady fringes.","Fringe width rises with λ and D, and falls with d. A thin plate shifts the fringes but does not change their width."]},"7.9":{"s":"Diffraction is the spreading of light into the shadow region at the edges of an obstacle or slit; it is clear only when the slit is about as small as the wavelength. Fraunhofer diffraction uses plane wavefronts (lenses), Fresnel diffraction does not. A single slit gives a broad bright central band with weaker bands on each side; the central band is twice as wide as the others. Young's fringes are equally bright; single slit bands get fainter.","f":[{"x":"a*sin(theta) = n*lambda","n":"Single slit of width a: n-th dark band (minimum).","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 7.9, p.178"},{"x":"a*sin(theta) = (n + 1/2)*lambda","n":"Approximate position of the n-th bright band.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 7.9, p.178"},{"x":"W_c = 2*lambda*D/a","n":"Width of the central bright band.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 7.9, p.178"}],"w":["In single slit diffraction a sin θ = nλ gives DARK bands; in Young's experiment path difference nλ gives BRIGHT fringes."]},"7.10":{"s":"Resolving power is the ability of an instrument to show two nearby objects as separate. Rayleigh's criterion: two objects are just resolved when the first minimum of one diffraction pattern falls on the central maximum of the other. The smallest resolvable angle or distance is the limit of resolution; resolving power is its reciprocal. A microscope resolves better with shorter wavelength and larger numerical aperture (oil immersion); a telescope with a larger objective.","f":[{"x":"d(theta) = lambda/a","n":"Limit of resolution for two slit-like (linear) objects; a = aperture width.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 7.10, p.181"},{"x":"R = (n*sin(alpha))/(0.61*lambda)","n":"Resolving power of a microscope for self-luminous points; n sin(alpha) = numerical aperture.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 7.10, p.182"},{"x":"theta = 1.22*lambda/D","n":"Telescope: smallest resolvable angle; D = diameter of the objective. Resolving power = D/(1.22 lambda).","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 7.10, p.183"}],"w":["Magnification does not improve resolution; only a larger aperture or shorter wavelength does."]}};
